---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PDFCompressor from '../components/PDFCompressor.astro';
import SEOContentPDF from '../components/SEOContentPDF.astro';

const SEO_TITLE = "Compress PDF to 50KB — Smallest Offline Compressor";
const SEO_DESC = "Reduce your PDF file to exactly 50KB. Perfect for online applications with strict limits. 100% free, fast, and secure with no server uploads required.";
---

<Layout title={SEO_TITLE} description={SEO_DESC}>
  <style is:global>
    :root {
      --color-lime: #f43f5e;
    }
  </style>

  <section class="relative z-20 pt-20 container-stripe">
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <div style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; background: rgba(244, 63, 94, 0.08); border: 1px solid rgba(244, 63, 94, 0.2); border-radius: 100px; padding: 5px 14px; margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <span style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--color-lime);">Free Image Compression</span>
      </div>

      <h1 style="font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 8vw, 4.5rem); line-height: 1; margin: 0 0 24px; letter-spacing: -0.05em;">
        Compress PDF <br/>
        <span style="color: var(--color-lime);">to 50KB.</span>
      </h1>
      <p style="color: var(--color-ink-2); font-size: 1.125rem; margin-bottom: 24px; max-width: 500px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; line-height: 1.6;">
        Instantly compress your PDF document to exactly 50KB for portal uploads. Processed 100% securely on your device.
      </p>
    </div>

    <PDFCompressor defaultTargetKB={50} />
  </section>

  <SEOContentPDF toolName="compress" />

  <!-- Comprehensive In-Depth SEO Guide Section -->
  <article class="pdf-guide-article" style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 80px auto 120px; padding: 0 24px; text-align: left; font-family: var(--font-body); color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.75;">
    <div style="height: 1px; background: linear-gradient(to right, transparent, var(--color-border), transparent); margin-bottom: 64px;"></div>

    <header style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <div style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; background: rgba(244, 63, 94, 0.08); border: 1px solid rgba(244, 63, 94, 0.2); border-radius: 100px; padding: 4px 12px; margin-bottom: 16px;">
        <span style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--color-lime);">Official Portal Compliance Guide</span>
      </div>
      <h2 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.75rem); font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: -0.03em; color: var(--color-ink); line-height: 1.2; margin: 0 0 20px;">
        The Definitive Guide to Compressing PDF to 50KB for Government Portals
      </h2>
      <p style="font-size: 1.125rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); max-width: 820px; margin: 0;">
        Shrinking a PDF document to exactly 50KB or below is one of the most frustrating hurdles when submitting online application forms across India. Whether you are dealing with Staff Selection Commission (SSC) signature attachments, UPPSC OTR document verification, or state recruitment certificates, learn how our client-side compression engine achieves ultra-low file sizes without sacrificing critical legibility.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- SECTION 1: The Challenge of 50KB Limit -->
    <section style="margin-bottom: 56px;">
      <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.625rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        The Technical Challenge: Why Compressing a PDF to 50KB Is Exceptionally Tough
      </h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        To understand why compressing a PDF to 50KB (precisely 51,200 bytes) poses such an intense technical obstacle, it helps to dissect how the Portable Document Format operates internally. Unlike raw image files such as JPEG or WebP, a PDF is not merely an image wrapper; it is an object-oriented compound document container.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        Every standard PDF file contains mandatory structural overhead. This includes the PDF file header, the trailer dictionary, document catalog objects, font descriptor tables, page layout trees, and the critical cross-reference table (known as the <code>xref</code> table), which indexes every byte offset in the file. Before a single character of candidate text, rubber stamp, or pen stroke is rendered, these architectural structures alone consume between <strong>8KB and 25KB</strong> of raw data.
      </p>
      <div style="background: var(--color-surface-2); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-lime); border-radius: 0 12px 12px 0; padding: 20px 24px; margin: 28px 0;">
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--color-ink);">
          <strong>The Shrinking Data Budget:</strong> When a government portal establishes a hard ceiling of 50KB, your actual usable file budget for visual document content is restricted to approximately <strong>25KB to 38KB</strong>. Trying to fit high-resolution scanned text into this tiny allocation using conventional desktop software almost always triggers compression artifacts or exceeds the threshold.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        Standard online PDF compressors typically fail at this task. Most cloud-based tools rely on standard Ghostscript presets (like <code>/screen</code> or <code>/ebook</code>), which bottom out around 80KB to 150KB. When forced further, they ruthlessly downsample images into an unrecognizable, blurry pixel soup that automated OCR validators and government scrutiny officers instantly reject. Our browser-based engine was built specifically to solve this precision challenge.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 2: Portals Requiring 50KB PDFs -->
    <section style="margin-bottom: 56px;">
      <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.625rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        Which Recruitment & Government Portals Require 50KB PDFs?
      </h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        India's competitive exam systems process millions of applications in compressed windows. To prevent server infrastructure crashes and conserve petabytes of cloud storage, government exam bodies enforce strict upload ceilings. The 50KB PDF limit is standard across numerous prominent central and state portals:
      </p>

      <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 20px; margin-bottom: 32px;">
        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
          <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <span style="display: inline-block; width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--color-lime);"></span>
            <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0;">SSC Signatures & Certificates</h4>
          </div>
          <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            While major exams like SSC CGL and CHSL generally use JPG for initial profile signatures, specialized SSC Selection Posts, departmental tests, and Post-Examination Document Verification (DV) regularly require candidate signature PDFs and discharge/caste certificates between <strong>20KB and 50KB</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
          <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <span style="display: inline-block; width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--color-lime);"></span>
            <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0;">UPPSC (Uttar Pradesh)</h4>
          </div>
          <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            The UPPSC One-Time Registration (OTR) system and subsequent PCS/RO/ARO vacancy application modules demand that domicile certificates, sports certificates, and category proofs be uploaded as individual PDF files not exceeding <strong>50KB</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
          <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <span style="display: inline-block; width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--color-lime);"></span>
            <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0;">BPSC & Bihar State Exams</h4>
          </div>
          <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            The Bihar Public Service Commission (BPSC), particularly during Teacher Recruitment Examination (TRE) stages and combined competitive examination rounds, requires scanned identity slips, non-creamy layer (NCL) certificates, and self-declarations strictly capped at <strong>50KB to 100KB</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
          <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <span style="display: inline-block; width: 10px; height: 10px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--color-lime);"></span>
            <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0;">State PSCs & MP Online</h4>
          </div>
          <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            WBPSC (West Bengal), MPSC (Maharashtra), OSSC (Odisha), and MP Online (ESB / Vyapam) portals enforce strict upload validation scripts. Submitting a 51KB file results in an instant <em>"File size exceeded maximum limit of 50KB"</em> rejection.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style="overflow-x: auto; margin: 32px 0; border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px;">
        <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; text-align: left; font-size: 0.9rem;">
          <thead>
            <tr style="background: var(--color-surface-2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); color: var(--color-ink);">
              <th style="padding: 14px 18px;">Portal / Examination</th>
              <th style="padding: 14px 18px;">Document Type</th>
              <th style="padding: 14px 18px;">Mandatory Range</th>
              <th style="padding: 14px 18px;">Permitted Format</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">SSC Selection Posts & DV</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Candidate Signature / Experience Slips</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">20 KB – 50 KB</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">PDF / JPG</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">UPPSC OTR Portal</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Caste, Domicile & Special Category Proof</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Max 50 KB</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">PDF only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">BPSC Teacher / Civil Services</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Identity Proof, Affidavit & Declarations</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Max 50 KB</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">PDF only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">IBPS / SBI Specialist Officers</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Scribe Undertaking Form & NOC</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Max 50 KB</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">PDF only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink);">State Admission (DTE / CET)</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">Income Certificate, Bonafide Slip</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">20 KB – 50 KB</td>
              <td style="padding: 14px 18px;">PDF only</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 3: Single-Page vs Multi-Page Considerations -->
    <section style="margin-bottom: 56px;">
      <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.625rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        Single-Page vs. Multi-Page Considerations: The Mathematical Reality
      </h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        One of the most critical factors candidates overlook when preparing PDF files is page count. The laws of digital data compression dictate how much visual information can physically fit within 51,200 bytes:
      </p>

      <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 24px; margin: 28px 0;">
        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-2); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
          <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 12px;">
            Single-Page Documents (Recommended for 50KB)
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.925rem; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            In a single-page PDF, the entire 35KB–40KB image payload is dedicated to rendering one surface. This leaves ample bandwidth for our compressor to preserve razor-sharp text, government serial numbers, watermarks, and signature curves at high clarity.
          </p>
          <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;">
            <li><span style="color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 700;">✓</span> Ideal for single certificates, marksheet front-faces, signatures, or declarations.</li>
            <li><span style="color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 700;">✓</span> Text remains crisp down to 8-point font sizes.</li>
            <li><span style="color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 700;">✓</span> Official government round stamps and barcodes remain machine-readable.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-2); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
          <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 12px;">
            Multi-Page Documents (2 to 5 Pages)
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.925rem; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            When you attempt to cram 3 or 4 pages into 50KB, each page is throttled to approximately <strong>10KB to 12KB</strong>. At that extreme compression level, rasterized text breaks down into illegible pixelation, making dates and roll numbers unreadable.
          </p>
          <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;">
            <li><span style="color: #ef4444; font-weight: 700;">⚠</span> Not recommended to compress 3+ page documents to 50KB.</li>
            <li><span style="color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 700;">💡 Solution:</span> Use our <a href="/separate-pdf-pages" style="color: var(--color-lime); text-decoration: underline;">Separate PDF Pages</a> tool to extract only the qualifying semester or final degree certificate.</li>
            <li><span style="color: var(--color-lime); font-weight: 700;">💡 Alternative:</span> If a 2-sided document is required, scan both sides side-by-side onto a single A4 page before compressing.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 4: Scanning Best Practices -->
    <section style="margin-bottom: 56px;">
      <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.625rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        Scanning Best Practices: Setting Up Your Source Document for Maximum 50KB Quality
      </h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        A compression algorithm can only optimize what you feed into it. The secret to obtaining a crystalline 50KB PDF lies in the initial digitization stage. By implementing these expert scanning settings, you eliminate redundant digital noise before compression starts:
      </p>

      <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; margin-bottom: 28px;">
        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px 24px;">
          <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 8px;">
            1. Always Scan in 8-Bit Grayscale (Avoid 24-Bit RGB Color)
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0;">
            A full-color scan records three color channels (Red, Green, Blue) using 24 bits per pixel. Since certificates, income proofs, and signatures consist solely of black or blue ink on paper, storing color information wastes <strong>66% of the raw data budget</strong> on background yellowing and scanner illumination tint. Switching your mobile scanner app (like Adobe Scan, Document Scanner, or flatbed hardware) to <strong>Grayscale</strong> instantly cuts the file weight by two-thirds without losing any text sharpness.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px 24px;">
          <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 8px;">
            2. The 150 DPI Sweet Spot (Why 300 DPI Is a Trap)
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0;">
            Candidates often assume higher DPI is always better. However, scanning an A4 document at 300 DPI produces an uncompressed image of 2480 × 3508 pixels (~8.7 million pixels). Forcing 8.7 million pixels into 50KB forces the compression algorithm to destroy fine details. In contrast, <strong>150 DPI</strong> generates an optimal 1240 × 1754 pixel canvas. This provides crisp, print-standard clarity on portal review screens while fitting comfortably into the 50KB envelope.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px 24px;">
          <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 8px;">
            3. Tight Margin Cropping
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0;">
            When photographing documents with a smartphone, candidates frequently capture surrounding elements—bedsheets, dark desk wood grain, or fingertips. These high-entropy background textures consume valuable compression bits. Always crop tightly to the four document corners so that 100% of the byte allocation is spent on text.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px 24px;">
          <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 8px;">
            4. High-Contrast Black & White Binarization for Signatures
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0;">
            If you are generating a 50KB signature PDF for SSC or state PSC portals, sign on clean unruled white paper with black ink. In your scanner app, select the "Document / B&W / High Contrast" filter. This turns the paper background into solid pure white, which compresses virtually down to zero bytes, allocating maximum fidelity to the dark pen strokes.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 5: How Our Tool Achieves 50KB Without Destroying Readability -->
    <section style="margin-bottom: 56px;">
      <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.625rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        How Our Tool Achieves 50KB Without Destroying Readability
      </h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        Unlike primitive converters that simply degrade resolution blindly, our PDF compressor employs an intelligent four-stage client-side optimization pipeline designed specifically for official document submissions:
      </p>

      <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 20px; margin-bottom: 32px;">
        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-2); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
          <span style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-lime); display: block; margin-bottom: 8px;">PIPELINE STAGE 01</span>
          <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 10px;">
            Dynamic Canvas Rasterization
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            The tool ingests your PDF directly in browser memory via a WebAssembly-compiled PDF engine. It calculates the optimal viewport scale (scaling up to 1.5x on desktop) to retain fine font serifs, stamp borders, and signatures prior to encoding.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-2); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
          <span style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-lime); display: block; margin-bottom: 8px;">PIPELINE STAGE 02</span>
          <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 10px;">
            Iterative Binary-Search Calibration
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            Rather than guessing quality factors, our engine runs an automated 8-iteration binary search across JPEG quantization tables (evaluating discrete steps between 0.05 and 0.95). It discovers the mathematical peak of clarity that remains strictly under 51,200 bytes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-2); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
          <span style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-lime); display: block; margin-bottom: 8px;">PIPELINE STAGE 03</span>
          <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 10px;">
            Safe Post-EOF Byte Alignment
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            Certain NIC and state portal validation scripts reject documents that fall short of minimum size requirements (e.g., "file must be between 20KB and 50KB"). If an aggressively compressed file drops below target, our engine safely appends non-destructive whitespace and comment bytes after the final <code>%%EOF</code> marker to land on the requested target without corrupting the PDF structure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div style="background: var(--color-surface-2); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 24px;">
          <span style="font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-lime); display: block; margin-bottom: 8px;">PIPELINE STAGE 04</span>
          <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.15rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 10px;">
            100% Zero-Server Privacy
          </h4>
          <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">
            Unlike commercial utilities that upload your government certificates, Aadhaar slips, or PAN cards to foreign cloud servers, our tool processes everything locally in your device RAM. Your private identity documents never cross the internet.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- SECTION 6: Step-by-Step Instructions -->
    <section style="margin-bottom: 56px;">
      <h3 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.625rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 20px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        Step-by-Step Instructions: Compressing Your PDF to 50KB
      </h3>
      <ol style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px;">
        <li style="display: flex; gap: 16px; background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px;">
          <div style="flex-shrink: 0; width: 36px; height: 36px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--color-surface-3); border: 1px solid var(--color-border-mid); display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-lime);">1</div>
          <div>
            <h4 style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-ink); margin: 0 0 6px;">Upload Your Document</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.925rem; margin: 0;">Drag and drop your PDF file into the upload zone above, or tap <strong>"Browse files"</strong> to select the document from your computer or smartphone storage.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li style="display: flex; gap: 16px; background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px;">
